<Project>
  <!--
    Applies to every project under tests/. Imports the repo-root props first so the version,
    analyzer and audit settings there still apply — without this the nearest-file-wins rule would
    silently drop all of them for the test projects.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Pin test output to bin\$(Configuration)\ regardless of $(Platform).

      MSBuild's default output path is bin\$(Platform)\$(Configuration)\ whenever Platform is
      anything but AnyCPU. The WinUI app genuinely needs -p:Platform=x64, and the documented build
      command passes it at the solution — so it is a very small step to also pass it to a
      `dotnet test`, at which point the test assembly is written to bin\x64\Debug\ while the plain
      solution build wrote it to bin\Debug\. Both directories then exist, and a `dotnet test`
      passing that platform plus the no-build switch silently runs whichever stale copy is sitting
      in the x64 one.

      That is not hypothetical. It reported a passing suite as one failure against an AutomationId
      that no longer exists in the source, and separately reported the integration suite as 92
      tests when it actually has 164 — a wrong answer in both directions, from a months-old binary,
      with nothing in the output hinting that the run was stale.

      CI dodges this by never passing -p:Platform to a test step (see the BUILD_PLATFORM note in
      ci.yml). This makes the dodge unnecessary: with one output path per configuration, passing
      the platform or not resolves to the same assembly, so there is no second copy to go stale.
    -->
    <AppendPlatformToOutputPath>false</AppendPlatformToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      xUnit1051 — "pass TestContext.Current.CancellationToken to methods that accept one".

      New in xunit v3 and it fires 412 times across the four suites, because every existing
      `await something(ct)` call site predates the rule. The repo builds warning-clean, so leaving
      it on would mean either 412 warnings permanently or a 412-call-site mechanical edit folded
      into the v3 migration commit — and a diff that large, touching only argument lists, is one
      nobody can meaningfully review. A real defect hidden in it would ship.

      The rule is a responsiveness improvement, not a correctness one: it lets the runner cancel a
      test that is blocked mid-await. That matters for long or hanging tests; these suites finish
      in seconds. Suppressed here rather than per-file so the decision is in one place, and so
      removing this line is all it takes to adopt the rule later — ideally per project, as a
      change of its own.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);xUnit1051</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
